# ABI i386 threads: a new thread's entry state
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Source: the i386 psABI, "Low Level System Information", Process
# Initialization / Thread State (a new thread inherits the creating thread's
# floating-point state, which is private to it afterwards) and the function
# entry rules of the calling sequence. The start routine is entered through a
# recording trampoline so the state at its first instruction is seen.
#

assertion  ABI.i386.threads.001
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/The Stack Frame (sec-stack-frame)
class      required
says       The start routine of a new thread is entered like any function: ESP plus 4
           is at least a multiple of 4.
note       The part of the entry rule that holds in both revisions; see threads.002.
test       t/001-start-routine-word-aligned.c
nomutant   the behaviour belongs to the thread library and the system; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.threads.002
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/The Stack Frame (sec-stack-frame)
class      disputed
says       The start routine of a new thread is entered with ESP plus 4 a multiple of
           16.
note       Follows the current revision (16 bytes); the older one asks for word
           alignment only, so a FAIL is INSPECT.
test       t/002-start-routine-16.c
nomutant   the behaviour belongs to the thread library and the system; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.threads.003
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Registers (table fig-reg-usage)
class      required
says       The direction flag is clear at every function entry, so at the start routine
           of a new thread.
note       Both revisions.
test       t/003-start-routine-df-clear.c
nomutant   the behaviour belongs to the thread library and the system; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.threads.004
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Process Initialization/Thread State
class      required
says       A new thread inherits the floating-point state of its creator: with the
           creator's x87 control word set to round-down, the thread's start routine is
           entered with it.
note       Current revision only.
test       t/004-x87-cw-inherited.c
nomutant   the behaviour belongs to the thread library and the system; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.threads.005
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Process Initialization/Thread State
class      required
says       A new thread inherits the creator's MXCSR: with the creator's rounding set
           to round-down, the thread's start routine is entered with it.
note       Current revision only.
test       t/005-mxcsr-inherited.c
nomutant   the behaviour belongs to the thread library and the system; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.threads.006
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Process Initialization/Thread State
class      required
says       A thread started from the process's initial floating-point state (x87
           control word 0x037F, MXCSR control bits 0x1F80) begins in that state.
note       Inferred from inheritance (Thread State) and the process-entry tables;
           current revision.
test       t/006-default-fp-state.c
nomutant   the behaviour belongs to the thread library and the system; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.threads.007
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Process Initialization/Thread State
class      required
says       The floating-point state is private to each thread after creation: a thread
           that sets round-down for itself leaves its creator's control words
           unchanged, and a thread started afterwards does not see the change.
note       Current revision only.
test       t/007-fp-state-private.c
nomutant   the behaviour belongs to the thread library and the system; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh
